60
00:02:04.779 --> 00:02:06.838
this consistent would almost never come

61
00:02:06.838 --> 00:02:09.051
from luck: its p, a number the next

62
00:02:09.051 --> 00:02:10.492
chapter explains, is tiny.

63
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.653
Thrown into two unrelated piles and

64
00:02:12.653 --> 00:02:14.609
compared with a different test, the

65
00:02:14.609 --> 00:02:16.925
Mann-Whitney U test, the same numbers give

66
00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:19.189
a p millions of times larger, because the

67
00:02:19.189 --> 00:02:20.785
song-to-song spread is left in.

68
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.632
Are these differences really above zero,

69
00:02:26.632 --> 00:02:28.183
or could luck give the same?

70
00:02:28.516 --> 00:02:31.119
Take the row five page's five songs: plus

71
00:02:31.119 --> 00:02:33.723
point three one, plus point one two, minus

72
00:02:33.723 --> 00:02:36.271
point zero two, plus point two five, plus

73
00:02:36.271 --> 00:02:37.158
point four zero.

74
00:02:37.616 --> 00:02:40.205
Step one: sort them by size, ignoring the

75
00:02:40.205 --> 00:02:40.470
sign.

76
00:02:40.787 --> 00:02:42.213
Step two: number them.

77
00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.855
The smallest, point zero two, gets rank

78
00:02:44.855 --> 00:02:47.445
one; the biggest, point four zero, rank

79
00:02:47.445 --> 00:02:47.709
five.

80
00:02:48.347 --> 00:02:50.611
Step three: add up the ranks of the

81
00:02:50.611 --> 00:02:51.771
positive differences.

82
00:02:52.102 --> 00:02:54.588
T plus, the sum, over songs where d i is

83
00:02:54.588 --> 00:02:57.239
above zero, of each song's rank R i: two

84
00:02:57.239 --> 00:02:59.614
plus three plus four plus five, fourteen.

85
00:02:59.945 --> 00:03:01.437
The negatives get only one.

86
00:03:01.768 --> 00:03:04.088
Big ranks on one side is what a real lean

87
00:03:04.088 --> 00:03:04.695
looks like.

88
00:03:06.457 --> 00:03:07.719
Now the null hypothesis.

89
00:03:08.035 --> 00:03:10.138
Wilcoxon's null is that the differences

90
00:03:10.138 --> 00:03:12.609
are spread evenly around zero: a negative

91
00:03:12.609 --> 00:03:14.817
difference is as likely as a positive one

92
00:03:14.817 --> 00:03:15.711
of the same size.

93
00:03:16.026 --> 00:03:18.234
That's what you'd expect if the loop were

94
00:03:18.234 --> 00:03:18.918
just as good.

95
00:03:19.233 --> 00:03:20.705
So each sign is a coin flip.

96
00:03:21.021 --> 00:03:22.914
Five coins give thirty-two patterns.

97
00:03:23.229 --> 00:03:25.122
Here's T plus for every one of them.

98
00:03:25.531 --> 00:03:27.847
Only two patterns reach fourteen or more.

99
00:03:28.186 --> 00:03:30.390
Two more are as lopsided the other way.

100
00:03:30.729 --> 00:03:33.158
So the p-value is four in thirty-two:

101
00:03:33.158 --> 00:03:34.232
point one two five.

102
00:03:34.703 --> 00:03:36.832
One time in eight, luck alone gives

103
00:03:36.832 --> 00:03:39.354
something this lopsided or more, in either

104
00:03:39.354 --> 00:03:39.914
direction.

105
00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:41.371
That's what p means.

106
00:03:41.707 --> 00:03:44.005
It is not the chance that the loop is as

107
00:03:44.005 --> 00:03:46.358
good, and it says nothing about how big

108
00:03:46.358 --> 00:03:47.367
the difference is.

109
00:03:47.703 --> 00:03:50.057
And with only five songs it can never get

110
00:03:50.057 --> 00:03:52.578
below point zero six two five, even if all

111
00:03:52.578 --> 00:03:53.587
five are positive.

112
00:03:53.923 --> 00:03:56.165
The code calls the same test, SciPy's

113
00:03:56.165 --> 00:03:58.350
wilcoxon, on the list of differences.

114
00:03:58.686 --> 00:04:01.152
The real runs have more than fifty songs,

115
00:04:01.152 --> 00:04:03.169
